# maxi-config-owned ShellCheck configuration.
#
# Distributed as .shellcheckrc by scripts/sync-maxi-review.sh --manifest
# review-configs. Edit it HERE, not in the target repo.
#
# NOT every org repo, despite what this header used to say. The push inventory
# is the DECLARED repo list unioned with repos DISCOVERED by already carrying an
# owned maxi-fix.yml. Declared repos are included unconditionally; having no
# default-branch workflow only makes a repo undiscoverable, not excluded. What
# is excluded is a repo that is neither declared nor discoverable -- a newly
# created one, until someone adds it to the list. Such a repo gets neither these
# settings nor the warning below. See maxi-tools/maxi-config#319.
#
# Lives at the repo ROOT because Codacy detects ShellCheck config by that name.
# The SETTINGS below are kept identical to maxi-config's own
# .qlty/configs/.shellcheckrc so qlty, the Codacy CLI, the Codacy PR bot and a
# bare local `shellcheck` all behave the same way. Most consumer repos have no
# .qlty directory; the settings here stand on their own and that file is named
# only to say where the pair is kept in step.

# Resolve `source`/`.` relative to the script being checked rather than the
# invoking directory.
source-path=SCRIPTDIR

# source-path alone does not suppress SC1091. Measured on shellcheck 0.11.0,
# a script sourcing a sibling, invoked from outside its directory:
#
#   (no config)                  SC1091 not following: ./lib.sh was not
#                                       specified as input (see -x)
#   source-path=SCRIPTDIR        SC1091 not following: ./lib.sh was not
#                                       specified as input (see -x)
#   both settings                clean -- PROVIDED the sourced file exists on
#                                       disk when shellcheck runs
#
# The first two are byte-identical: source-path lets shellcheck FIND a sourced
# file, external-sources is what lets it FOLLOW one.
#
# That precondition is not a footnote. For anything generated at run time -- a
# venv activate script, a file a build step writes -- external-sources changes
# only the WORDING of SC1091, from "was not specified as input" to
# "openBinaryFile: does not exist (No such file or directory)". The finding
# stays. Silence those at the call site with `# shellcheck source=/dev/null`,
# not by expecting this file to do it.
#
# What external-sources actually costs, stated plainly because the previous
# version of this comment got it wrong: it lets shellcheck OPEN ANY FILE a
# script names in a `source` line -- absolute paths and upward traversal
# included. source-path does NOT confine that. It adds search directories for
# resolving RELATIVE sources; it is not a sandbox. Verified on 0.11.0 with both
# settings on: a script under inner/ sourcing `/tmp/outside.sh` and
# `../sibling.sh` reports no SC1091 for either, which is to say it followed
# both. This is why shellcheck keeps the setting off by default and refuses to
# honour it from inside a script -- it is a trust boundary, not a convenience.
#
# The trade is acceptable where the scripts are first-party and the machine
# running shellcheck holds nothing the repo does not: opening a sourced path
# then discloses nothing that reading the checkout already would.
#
# Note it is NOT justified by "CI runs these scripts anyway". In maxi-config it
# does not: hooks/pre-push and hooks/pre-commit-lint there are the LOCAL half
# of the lint policy and no job invokes them. Check your own repo rather than
# assuming either way.
#
# It is NOT made acceptable by a same-repo guard. A branch in the same repo can
# still edit a `source` line to name an arbitrary host file, and this fleet's
# written posture is that persistent self-hosted machines never execute
# pull-request code AT ALL -- including pull requests from trusted repository
# members -- because the threat is persistence, not token scope or fork origin:
#
#   maxi-config: ci/runner-routing.toml, [lanes.linux-x64-ephemeral-review]
#   maxi-config: docs/claude-review-routing.md
#
# (Both paths are in maxi-config, not in the repo you are reading this from.
# Same reason the issue reference above is spelled out in full: this file is
# copied into other repos, where a bare path or #number resolves locally and
# points at nothing.)
#
# So keep shellcheck off the pull-request path on persistent runners, or on an
# ephemeral lane. Do not read the same-repo predicate as sufficient.
external-sources=true
